host-neutral engine (decided, VD 2026-09-08; docs/decisions.md dec-B85)
  after the merge to main, before the 1.0-0 CRAN submission (VD
  2026-09-23: an interface change consumers build against, so it lands
  before the header becomes the 1.0 contract): internal plain-C spec
  stage in the bridge; the engine's Rmath touchpoints through
  src/external's ext_* names only (model.hpp calls Rf_pnorm5, Rf_dnorm4
  and Rf_dt directly today), the implementation chosen at build time -
  Rmath under R (bit-identical), a vendored copy of R's nmath pnorm,
  qnorm, dnorm and dt otherwise (VD 2026-09-24: no run-time reason for
  hooks, no SIMD variant and no BLAS use); qchisq stays host-side, the
  spec carrying the resolved residual-prior scale; printing likewise
  build-time (dec-A74): ext_printf, ext_fflush_stdout and the message
  and warning entries map per host (R console, stdio for a standalone
  build), and the unused misc_printf/misc_flushOutput pointers and
  their installers go; a plain-spec creation entry and a host-neutral
  error contract in dbarts.h. The sister re-verification at the freeze
  follows it. Rewords the Python-binding entry.
  Predictor source consolidation (decided, VD 2026-09-24; dec-B124),
  same arc: the spec's predictor part is dbarts_predictor_source, so the
  R parse and the flat entries fill one struct and pass one
  translate-and-validate stage (translateSource, today flat-only) into
  bartcore::PredictorSource. The header takes the engine's addressing:
  dbarts_predictor_source gains denseChannels (per column, >= 0 double
  column, < 0 code column ~k), denseCodes is read only through it, and
  the kind-selected single columnSources index goes. Retires the
  per-bridge bounds and channel sweeps and the int32 -> ColumnKind /
  xint_t copies; columnTypes and categoryCounts gain a reader. Refusals
  that name a column by its R name stay in the R bridge. Owed: the
  in-tree C consumer test, an ABI hash re-bake, a stan4bart rebuild
  (dense helper only, no source change).

frequency weights (decided, VD 2026-09-24; docs/decisions.md dec-B125)
  after the merge to main, before the 1.0-0 CRAN submission: an explicit
  opt-in to frequency weights (a row stands for w identical
  observations) beside the precision default, on the families where
  that is a coherent model. Gaussian is certain: sigma's posterior df
  nu + sum(w) over the weighted SSR, the sigest df sum(w) - p, the ppd
  and log-likelihood at sd sigma with each row counted w times; leaf
  updates unchanged. A design note first settles the family list
  (Student-t, heteroscedastic, AFT, probit and ordinal carry a per-row
  latent or scale, so w copies are not one row reweighted; logistic's
  counts are already binomial trials, a different thing), the
  argument's name and shape on bart, dbarts, the sampler and dbarts.h,
  whether non-integer frequency weights are refused, and zero weights
  (excluded, as now). The weights docs say which a weight is.

engine constants audit (decided, VD 2026-09-08; docs/decisions.md
  dec-B91; LANDED; plan docs/plans/engine-performance.md)
  Open: the sparse-layout test in inst/tinytest/test-engine-constants.R
  runs one sweep, where the layouts still agree bitwise; at twenty they
  differ by 6e-16 to 9e-16, so it should run twenty to discriminate them
  (measured 2026-09-10; the layout itself stays non-bitwise, dec-B117).
  post-release: a size gate on the fused roll + suffstat pass, two
  thresholds rather than one - fuse weighted at n >= 1e4, unweighted at
  n >= 2e4 - since the fusion loses up to 8 percent below those and
  wins up to 24 above, and one threshold cannot serve both families
  (at n = 1e4 it would have to fuse weighted and decline unweighted);
  leaf occupancy is a worse key than n and is not even monotone in the
  gain. It must arrive WITH equivalence scenarios sized above the
  threshold: every fit in all three corpora today is under 2000 rows,
  so a gate would revert all 75 to the stock association and leave the
  fused pass with no equivalence coverage at all. Crossover grid and
  corpus count: docs/plans/engine-performance.md, "Crossover grid for
  a size gate".

engine generics deep dive (decided, VD 2026-09-08; docs/decisions.md
  dec-B92)
  an independent design memo on what the engine's generic axes should
  be (leaf kind, response family, forest count and coupling, response
  shape, threading), derived from the model space the package intends
  to cover rather than from the current headers, with the current
  shape critiqued against it. Written: docs/design/engine-generics-
  review.md. VD (2026-09-08): not reading it now; turn it into research
  with measurements down the road. Unscheduled.

rule draw default weight (decided, VD 2026-09-08; docs/decisions.md
  dec-B96)
  stays at weight zero through the merge; the 0.16 was agent-suggested
  without rationale. The post-merge mixing research sets the weight with
  a rationale, pre- or post-1.0-0 as time allows. Manual documents the
  kernel and its cost.

binary-hyperprior-revisit (raised by the study, 2026-09-14;
  docs/plans/binary-hyperprior.md, "What to revisit after the mixing
  work"; post-release, gated on the mixing line of inquiry)
  the sampled k does not converge at any length that study could pay
  for: 64,000 draws per fit reach an effective sample size of 29 and a
  split-Rhat that never falls below 1.2. Three of its results rest on
  that - every sampled-k number in the note is a chain-length summary
  rather than a posterior one, the residual 0.008 of coverage
  shortfall still showing at the probe could be the sampler or the
  model, and the interior optimum is located by coverage-distance
  differences of 0.002 to 0.005, the same order as the 0.012 the
  incumbent's own coverage still moved between the long length and the
  probe, so the optimum's location is not resolved to better than
  about a factor of two in scale. To reopen the default, a mixing fix
  has to show two things: the sampled k at a split-Rhat below 1.05
  with an effective sample size in the hundreds at a length a user
  would actually run, and then, at that length, either a spread in
  coverage distance across the finite-scale arms larger than 0.02 or
  an optimum somewhere other than scale 0.5 to 2. If the spread stays
  at half a point of coverage, no mixing fix reopens the question; it
  only makes the verdict better founded. Re-run with
  benchmarks/R/binary-hyperprior.R. The fixed-k results, the largest
  effects in the note, involve no sampled k and do not depend on this.

BayesTree claim and build flags (decided, VD 2026-09-08;
  docs/decisions.md dec-B107; LANDED a98c4cad; plan
  docs/plans/interfaces-and-dependencies.md)
  configure.ac's --enable-match-bayes-tree, --enable-thread-safe-unload
  and --with-xint-size stop with a removal message for one release
  (1.0-0). Open: delete the three stubs outright, and configure.ac's
  comment naming them, in the release after 1.0-0.

auto-family-verbose (decided, VD 2026-09-26; docs/decisions.md
  dec-A16): before the merge, verbose = FALSE suppresses the message
  family = "auto" prints when it picks a family from a factor
  response (announceAutoFamily and its callers in R/bart.R and
  R/data.R); the manual's verbose and family entries say so.

chi-k-uncapped (decided, VD 2026-09-26; docs/decisions.md dec-A13):
  before the merge, ChiKHyperprior::draw returns the exact draw and
  maxDraw goes; draws below the old 1e6 ceiling are unchanged, so no
  baseline moves. test-binaryResponse-hyperprior.R's runaway block
  checks that a chain reaching an infinite k finishes with baseline
  fits and no NaN; confirm storeState, setState and warm.start accept
  an infinite k. The chi() entry in dbartsPriors.Rd says the
  infinite scale's posterior is improper and k can drift to
  infinity, flattening the fit.

constructor-vocabulary (decided, VD 2026-09-26; docs/decisions.md
  dec-A67): before the merge, interactions, blocks, forest and
  varianceForest leave the exports and are evaluated in vocabulary
  (evalInVocabulary) inside the arguments that take them, with one
  exported list for use outside a call, named in the dbartsPriors
  manner; the manual pages, examples, vignettes and the sister
  packages' calls follow; blocks stays beside interactions (dec-A56).

predict-na-action (decided, VD 2026-09-26; docs/decisions.md
  dec-B34): before the merge, predict gains na.action on every fit
  class's method. The default predicts rows whose missing values sit
  in columns that had missing values in training and refuses the
  rest by name, as today; na.pass gives NA for the unroutable rows and
  keeps the output aligned with newdata; na.omit drops any row with a
  missing value; na.fail errors on any. The manual sets these against
  the fit-time meanings (routable versus unroutable missingness).

reproducibility-docs (decided, VD 2026-09-26; docs/decisions.md
  dec-A04): before the merge, one user-facing Reproducibility section
  on bart's page that the package page, bartBT, dbarts, dbartsControl,
  xbart and the sampler class link to. Today the text sits in bartBT,
  the package page points at a section bart does not have, the
  sampler class page says nothing about generators, and bartBT's
  section carries an unrelated levelGibbs paragraph. Cover, each
  verified against the code: set.seed versus seed; chains and thread
  count; repeated run() calls, copy, save and setState on an embedded
  sampler; xbart's per-cell seeds; which helpers do advance R's
  stream; same draws within a host but not promised across hosts,
  compilers or dbarts versions, and not against 0.9-x.

setter-update-state (decided, VD 2026-09-26; docs/decisions.md
  dec-A14): before the merge, every dbartsSampler setter left without
  updateState follows control@updateState (NA -> control, as run and
  the prior draws do), restoring 0.9-x; the setters' docstrings and
  the sampler class manual drop "updateState is opt-in".

fitted argument order (decided, VD 2026-09-26; docs/decisions.md dec-A15)
  before the merge, fitted's ci.level moves after sample on bart fits,
  restoring 0.9-x's positional (object, type, sample); multinomial,
  ordinal and negbin fits gain sample (passed through to extract,
  which already serves their test channel), so every class with a test
  channel reads fitted(object, type, sample, ci.level, ...). hurdle
  stays (object, type, ci.level, ...) until it has a test channel.

hurdle feature completion (VD 2026-09-26: part of feature completion,
  not to be forgotten; docs/decisions.md dec-A15)
  a hurdle.lognormal fit takes no test set, weights, subset or offset:
  the R composition uses the positive part's test slot to hand that
  sampler the full training x. Owed: a real test channel (extract and
  fitted then take sample), and weights, subset and offset where the
  two-part model defines them. Schedule not yet set by VD.

